{
  "skillId": "geo-u3-cpctc",
  "lessonVersion": 1,
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "concept",
      "type": "Concept",
      "title": "CPCTC",
      "scene": "scene_cpctc_concept",
      "proseRef": "06-cpctc.md#concept"
    },
    {
      "id": "manipulate",
      "type": "Manipulate",
      "title": "Confirm SSS then read off a pair of angles",
      "scene": "scene_cpctc_manipulate",
      "instruction": "Drag point F until EF ≅ BC (the third pair of sides). Once all three side pairs match, the triangles are congruent by SSS. Then observe that ∠A and ∠D are corresponding angles — by CPCTC they must be congruent.",
      "successCondition": {
        "type": "equalLengths",
        "of": "EF",
        "and": "BC",
        "tolerance": 0.15
      },
      "successMessage": "SSS established! By CPCTC, every pair of corresponding parts is now congruent — including ∠A ≅ ∠D.",
      "maxAttempts": null
    },
    {
      "id": "worked",
      "type": "WorkedExample",
      "title": "Using CPCTC after SSS",
      "scene": "scene_cpctc_worked",
      "steps": [
        {
          "order": 1,
          "text": "Establish SSS: AB ≅ DE, BC ≅ EF, CA ≅ FD (all three pairs of sides congruent). This proves △ABC ≅ △DEF.",
          "canvasAction": { "type": "highlight", "target": "AB" }
        },
        {
          "order": 2,
          "text": "By CPCTC, every pair of corresponding parts is congruent. In particular, ∠A and ∠D are corresponding angles (A↔D in the congruence statement).",
          "canvasAction": { "type": "highlight", "target": "arcA" }
        },
        {
          "order": 3,
          "text": "Conclusion: ∠A ≅ ∠D by CPCTC. We could not state this directly from SSS alone — CPCTC is the bridge that lets us extract information about individual parts after proving congruence.",
          "canvasAction": { "type": "annotate", "target": "both_triangles", "text": "∠A ≅ ∠D (CPCTC)" }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "check",
      "type": "Check",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "cpctc-check-1",
          "kind": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "What does CPCTC stand for?",
          "choices": [
            "Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles are Congruent",
            "Congruent Parts Create Triangle Congruence",
            "Corresponding Points Create Triangle Congruence",
            "Congruent Parts of Congruent Triangles are Corresponding"
          ],
          "answerIndex": 0,
          "explanation": "CPCTC = Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles are Congruent. It is used AFTER triangles have been proven congruent."
        },
        {
          "id": "cpctc-check-2",
          "kind": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "In a proof, when can you invoke CPCTC?",
          "choices": [
            "At the very first step",
            "Only after you have proven the two triangles are congruent",
            "Whenever two sides look equal in a diagram",
            "Before stating the congruence postulate"
          ],
          "answerIndex": 1,
          "explanation": "CPCTC is a consequence of triangle congruence — it cannot be used until the triangles have been proven congruent by SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, or HL."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "practice",
      "type": "Practice",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "cpctc-prac-1",
          "kind": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "△ABC ≅ △DEF by SAS (with the correspondence A↔D, B↔E, C↔F). Which pair of parts is congruent by CPCTC?",
          "choices": [
            "∠A ≅ ∠D",
            "AB ≅ EF",
            "∠B ≅ ∠F",
            "CA ≅ DE"
          ],
          "answerIndex": 0,
          "hints": [
            "CPCTC says corresponding parts are congruent. Corresponding means matching under the given vertex pairing.",
            "A corresponds to D — so ∠A corresponds to ∠D.",
            "∠A ≅ ∠D by CPCTC (since A↔D in the congruence statement)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "cpctc-prac-2",
          "kind": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "A student writes in a proof: 'BC ≅ EF by CPCTC.' What must appear earlier in the proof?",
          "choices": [
            "That BC and EF look equal in the figure",
            "That the two triangles containing BC and EF are congruent",
            "That ∠B ≅ ∠E",
            "That all six parts of both triangles are equal"
          ],
          "answerIndex": 1,
          "hints": [
            "CPCTC is a reason that follows from triangle congruence.",
            "You cannot use CPCTC unless the triangles have been proven congruent first.",
            "The proof must first establish △___ ≅ △___ by a congruence postulate/theorem."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "cpctc-prac-3",
          "kind": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "△ABD ≅ △CBD by HL. By CPCTC, which of the following is true?",
          "choices": [
            "∠ABD ≅ ∠CBD",
            "AD ≅ CD",
            "Both A and B",
            "Neither — HL does not allow CPCTC"
          ],
          "answerIndex": 2,
          "hints": [
            "CPCTC applies after ANY valid congruence proof, including HL.",
            "A↔C and B↔B. So ∠ABD ↔ ∠CBD and AD ↔ CD.",
            "Both ∠ABD ≅ ∠CBD and AD ≅ CD follow from CPCTC."
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "cpctc-prac-4",
          "kind": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "A proof concludes: △PQR ≅ △STU by ASA. The goal is to show QR ≅ TU. Is CPCTC valid here?",
          "choices": [
            "Yes — QR and TU are corresponding sides (Q↔T, R↔U)",
            "No — ASA only proves angle congruence",
            "No — CPCTC only works after SSS",
            "Yes — but only if QR and TU were part of the ASA setup"
          ],
          "answerIndex": 0,
          "hints": [
            "CPCTC works after ANY valid congruence proof.",
            "Under the correspondence P↔S, Q↔T, R↔U, the side QR corresponds to TU.",
            "Yes — △PQR ≅ △STU by ASA means all six pairs of parts are congruent, including QR ≅ TU."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reflection",
      "type": "Reflection",
      "proseRef": "06-cpctc.md#reflection"
    }
  ],
